---
// firma2-settings-account — the two facts about this account that its holder
// owns, and a line naming who owns the rest.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, and this is the whole design of the section.
// A ProjectFirma account carries four obvious fields: name, email, organization,
// role. Only the first two are the reader's to change — organization and role
// are assignments a tenant administrator makes, and they decide what this person
// can see and edit. Rendering all four as rows would have been the easy version
// and it would have LIED: every field here is a live control, and two of those
// four would have to refuse or silently revert. A control that offers an edit
// it cannot honour is worse than no control.
//
// So the section shows the two it can honour, and the note under them states
// the other two as a fact rather than faking them, and offers the list of
// administrators to ask (firma2-administrators-note). That also answers the product brief's
// "who is NOT here": the administrator, whose decisions this reader is living
// inside. Naming them in one line is cheaper than a disabled row that says the
// same thing in the shape of a broken affordance.
//
// A FORM, NOT A RECORD. This app has two field patterns and this section is
// the second one:
//
//   record  a page that READS first and can be corrected in place — the
//           project detail's Key facts. firma2-editable-field: no edge at rest,
//           the ring on hover, the control on click.
//   form    a surface whose only job is to be filled in — settings. The legos
//           as they ship: label above, border drawn at rest, value already in
//           the control. Nothing to discover, because the box IS the content.
//
// A settings screen is read by someone who came to change something, so a
// borderless value that only turns into a field on hover makes them hunt for
// the input. Hence esa-text-field directly, with its own visible label.
//
// NO CARD. Settings sections are unboxed (the spoke's settings-surface rule):
// a heading and a step of space say where the section starts, and the page's
// narrow reading column already bounds it — a border would be a second box.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: firma2-rail-section is the section and esa-text-field the
// control — both existing, composed unchanged. `.stack` spaces them; no bespoke
// flex, and no <style> block.
import Firma2RailSection from '../shared/firma2-rail-section.astro';
import Firma2AdministratorsNote from './firma2-administrators-note.astro';

// Mock, and invented — the same fictional person AppLayout's account menu names,
// read from the directory so the shell, this screen and Workspace settings ›
// Users agree about whose settings these are.
import { CURRENT_USER as account } from '../../data/firma2-directory';
---
<Firma2RailSection title="Account">
  {/* `md` on both — the spoke's one control rung — and the gap the Key facts
      panel uses between pairs, so the rhythm matches the record pattern even
      though the chrome does not. */}
  <div class="stack" data-gap="md">
    <esa-text-field name="name" label="Name" value={account.name} size="md" autocomplete="name" required></esa-text-field>
    <esa-text-field name="email" label="Email" value={account.email} size="md" type="email" autocomplete="email" required></esa-text-field>
  </div>

  {/* body-sm, the weight firma2-workspace-general gives the same kind of note:
      present for the reader who came looking for the organization row, quieter
      than the two fields above it. */}
  <Firma2AdministratorsNote />
</Firma2RailSection>

<script>
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-text-field';
</script>
